Runtime subset: hybrid PIC, scheduling, model/cache management, Qwen3.5 dense/MoE,
Bailing/Ring and KimiLinear models with their imported base implementations,
and their CUDA/Triton/JIT code and headers.

KVBench adaptations: private Python and Torch-op namespaces; native token-ID
requests with explicit validated segment ranges; native seam parameter;
restricted hybrid config/model registries compatible with Transformers 4.57;
local RoPE normalization instead of global Transformers patch hooks;
Cached-token metrics count all matched input tokens, including LinearKV/HYPIC
repair rows. Local compilation of the retained upstream native ops targets the
project PyTorch ABI.

Native norm.cu and pytorch_extension_utils.h are from FlashInfer commit
bc29697ba20b7e6bdb728ded98f04788e16ee021 (Apache-2.0), the dependency pinned
by the upstream sgl-kernel CMakeLists.txt. FlashInfer's Python package supplies
the accompanying CUDA template headers.

LinearKV is this branch's paper-based implementation, initially with a static
chunk-prefix selector, not the LinearKV authors' official runtime. The new recurrent replay
path supports Qwen GDN only. Upstream documents the conv-tail interpretation
and its need for model-level validation.

KVBench extends LinearKV selection with CacheBlend V-drift and ProphetKV
query-attention probes on the bundled backend. Probe effects stay request-private
and are restored before LB replay. EPIC partial repair excludes the chunk at
prompt position zero; full replay still includes it. These are local adapters,
not claims of upstream author-runtime performance equivalence.
